resolv.conf(5) allows '#' and ';' comments, and udhcpc's script tags the
entries it adds with the interface they came from so it can drop them again:

    nameserver 192.168.0.1 # eth0

parse_resolvconf() takes everything up to the newline as the argument, so the
comment becomes part of the server address and every lookup fails with

    nslookup: bad address '192.168.0.1 # eth0'

while musl's resolver, which stops at the first space, resolves fine. The same
truncation applies to "search" and "domain".
---
--- a/networking/nslookup.c
+++ b/networking/nslookup.c
@@ -721,6 +721,9 @@ static void parse_resolvconf(void)
 			char *p, *arg;
 			char *tokstate;
 
+			line[strcspn(line, "#;")] = '\0';
+			trim(line);
+
 			p = strtok_r(line, " \t\n", &tokstate);
 			if (!p)
 				continue;
